{"updated_at": {"date": {"d": "06", "m": "11", "Y": "2026"}, "time": {"H": "03", "M": "59", "S": "04"}}, "news": [{"title": "Haryana proposes FAR up to 264% for residential plots, S+4 height limit", "para": "The Haryana government is initiating amendments to the Haryana Building Code, which would enhance construction opportunities. Under the proposed changes, residential plots of up to 250 square metres would be eligible for a maximum floor area ratio of 264%. This modification is intended to assist homeowners wishing to remodel or expand their homes, as well as help developers.", "href": "https://realty.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/residential/haryana-proposes-far-up-to-264-for-residential-plots-s4-height-limit/135592857?utm_source=newslisting&utm_medium=latestNews", "img": "data:image/png;base64, ", "img-src": "https://etimg.etb2bimg.com/thumb/img-size-320232/135592857.cms?width=150&height=112", "tag": "residential"}, {"title": "Credai urges Goa government to speed up real estate project approvals", "para": "Real estate developers in Goa are facing mounting frustrations due to prolonged approval processes. The Confederation of Real Estate Developers\u2019 Associations of India points out serious inefficiencies within the bureaucratic framework. Navigating various departments for essential certificates can extend project timelines by several months. While some improvements are seen, significant obstacles still hinder approvals, underscoring the need for streamlined processes to enhance the business environment in the region.", "href": "https://realty.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/industry/credai-urges-goa-government-to-speed-up-real-estate-project-approvals/135592770?utm_source=newslisting&utm_medium=latestNews", "img": "data:image/png;base64, ", "img-src": "https://etimg.etb2bimg.com/thumb/img-size-240688/135592770.cms?width=150&height=112", "tag": "industry"}, {"title": "Haryana RERA rejects compensation plea, says buyers who stay on get only delay interest", "para": "The Haryana Real Estate Regulatory Authority addressed a compensation claim from a homebuyer in Gurgaon, who pursued over Rs 54 lakh due to losses stemming from possession delays. While a previous order required the builder to pay interest for these delays, HRera concluded that additional damages were unwarranted, citing established legal precedents that validate the pre-awarded compensation.", "href": "https://realty.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/rera/haryana-rera-rejects-compensation-plea-says-buyers-who-stay-on-get-only-delay-interest/135592684?utm_source=newslisting&utm_medium=latestNews", "img": "data:image/png;base64, ", "img-src": "https://etimg.etb2bimg.com/thumb/img-size-78064/135592684.cms?width=150&height=112", "tag": "rera"}]}